WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:04.190
Many of the biggest movies in history have
gone through extensive changes before they

00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:05.260
hit the silver screen.

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:09.720
Usually, the reasons for these changes are
easy enough to understand, but sometimes things

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:11.840
can take a turn for the truly bizarre.

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:16.860
Considered by many fans and critics to be
a minor misstep in the long-running James

00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:21.140
Bond series, Licence to Kill was the first
Bond movie not to take its name directly from

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:22.700
one of Ian Fleming's stories.

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:28.260
Instead, its title is an obvious reference
to James Bond's famous MI6 license to smoke

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:29.300
anyone he felt like.

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:34.800
The problem is that the crux of the movie's
story is that Bond isn't an MI6 agent anymore,

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:36.940
something the film's original title alluded
to.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:41.380
"When you're up to your ankles, you're gonna
beg to tell me everything.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:44.340
When you're up to your knees, you'll kiss
my ass to kill you."

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:50.500
Initially the movie was supposed to be called
Licence Revoked as a not-too-subtle nod to

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:51.820
Bond's change of fortune.

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:52.660
The problem?

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:56.980
Early test audiences in America assumed that
the title referred to James Bond losing his

00:00:56.989 --> 00:01:01.389
driver's license, which paints an altogether
more wholesome view of a movie in which Bond

00:01:01.389 --> 00:01:04.340
basically drops a dude into a glorified meat
grinder.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:09.240
Eager to avoid audiences making this mistake,
the studio arranged for a hasty title change,

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:12.670
which meant a bunch of marketing material
that had already been created featuring the

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:15.980
original name ended up being thrown straight
into the trash.

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:21.680
In early cuts of the 2012 remake of the '80s
teen adventure Red Dawn, the film revolved

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:25.360
around the concept of mainland America being
invaded by the Chinese.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:30.380
Originally slated for a 2010 release, the
movie was put on hold by MGM during a period

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:34.750
of financial turbulence at the studio, and
when Chinese state media caught wind of the

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:38.100
film via a leaked script, the situation went
from bad to worse.

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:43.020
China condemned the movie's themes, specifically
its plans to depict the nation as an invading

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:46.200
war machine that had to be stopped by a plucky
band of American kids.

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:50.040
"When I was overseas, we were the good guys,
we enforced order.

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:51.600
Well, now?

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:55.480
We're the bad guys, and we create chaos."

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:59.540
To appease Chinese censors and potentially
have the film screened for the increasingly

00:01:59.549 --> 00:02:04.310
lucrative Chinese box office, MGM paid to
have all the Chinese flags replaced with North

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:06.120
Korean ones using CGI.

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:11.060
Unfortunately, even after making the change,
the movie was doomed to Chinese exile, upon

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:14.880
the revised version's submission, censors
still refused to allow the movie to air in

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:15.380
China.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:20.680
During The Last Jedi, in addition to showing
off his swollen pecs and pasty Sithpack, Kylo

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:23.340
Ren debuts his personalized TIE silencer.

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:27.870
Outfitted with giant missiles, a giant laser
cannon and sporting an impossibly cool paint

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:32.370
job, the ship cuts an imposing silhouette
that reminds the audience, and Kylo Ren's

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:33.960
enemies, of the First Order's power.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:37.020
It was also changed because of a children's
toy.

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:42.460
In early concept art, Ren's TIE silencer sported
missiles that were housed on the ship's underside,

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:44.380
ready to be be deployed and fired as needed.

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:49.060
However, according to director Rian Johnson,
during production he received a prototype

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:53.400
toy version of the ship from Hasbro and noticed
that the missiles had mistakenly been mounted

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:54.160
on its wings.

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:59.340
Surmising that it'd be easier to just change
Ren's ship in the film using CGI rather than

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:03.980
redesign the toy itself, Johnson alerted the
VFX team about the alteration, and it was

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:06.020
corrected with the audience none the wiser.

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:11.640
Eraser is a mid-'90s Arnold Schwarzenegger
action film that received disappointing reviews.

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:15.611
Nevertheless, the film is chock-full of all
the schlocky Schwarzenegger-y goodness you'd

00:03:15.611 --> 00:03:20.050
expect from one of the star's films, including
an action scene during which he endlessly

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:24.860
fires two hyper-powerful future-guns into
a crowd of faceless bad guys and another that

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:27.900
finds him shooting a large CGI alligator in
the face.

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:32.300
"You're luggage."

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:37.640
The plot revolves around a company called
Cyrez that's trying to manufacture the aforementioned

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:39.510
future-guns to sell on the black market.

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:44.130
Originally, the company was called Cyrex because,
well, how futuristic does that sound?

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:48.430
However, just before the film was set to be
released, a Texan computer chip manufacturer

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:52.550
called Cyrix caught wind of it and threatened
to sue Warner Bros. because they didn't want

00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:56.060
to be associated with a fictional company
that made cool future-guns.

00:03:56.060 --> 00:04:01.620
Not wanting to fight the issue in court, Warner
Bros. begrudgingly spent a reported $1 million

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:04.460
changing the name to Cyrez with dubbing and
CGI.

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:06.420
Way to be spoil-sports, Cyrix.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:11.440
In the Stephen King novel that Stanley Kubrick's
The Shining is based on, most of the story's

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:13.680
action takes place in room 217.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:19.760
However, in the film adaptation, this is changed
to 237, which confused fans of the book, given

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:22.080
Kubrick's reputation for perfectionism.

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:26.340
As it turned out, while the change was made
by Kubrick, he was only acting on the wishes

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:29.320
of the Timberline Lodge, the hotel where the
movie was filmed.

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:35.470
The Timberline didn't have a room 237, but
they really did have a room 217, and management

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:39.440
didn't want their guests to be scared of staying
in that particular room after the movie was

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:39.940
released.

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:44.100
According to author James Howard, Kubrick
agreed to change the number largely as a favor

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:48.970
to the Lodge, and amusingly, in direct contrast
to their initial fears about the film and

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:53.440
its potential impact on the room's popularity,
the Timberline would later report that despite

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:58.510
Kubrick changing it, room 217 is still the
most commonly requested in the entire hotel.

